<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort Mose</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1738">1738</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Gracia Real de Santa Teresa de Mose</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fort Mose, or Gracia Real de Santa Teresa de Mose, was a fortified settlement north of St. Augustine in Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, founded in 1738 for Africans who had escaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in the British Carolinas and accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Led by the militia captain Francisco Menéndez, it was the first legally sanctioned free Black town in what became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Damaged in the British attack of 1740 and rebuilt, it was abandoned when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded Florida in 1763.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-mose.jpg" alt="Fort Mose" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ebyabe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Aug_Fort_Mose01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
